 "at": "2026-11-19T12:53:05",
 "reply": "{\"findings\": [{\"kind\": \"agreement\", \"text\": \"The founder's line is told the same way by Law and by the Puranic texts. Law says Saṃvaraṇa had a son Kuru, and the Agni, Brahma, Garuda, Harivamsa, Matsya, Mahabharata and Visnu texts all derive Kuru from Saṃvaraṇa. The Bhagavata names his mother as the Sun's daughter Tapatī.\", \"cites\": [\"l:8044\", \"g:0\", \"v:agni:AgP 277.26\", \"v:mbh1:MBh 1,89.42\", \"v:bhp9:BhP 9.22.4\"]}, {\"kind\": \"disagreement\", \"text\": \"Kuru's sons are named differently. Law gives Sudhanvan as Kuru's son. The Matsya names Sudhanvan and Jahnu as Kuru's sons, and the Bhagavata lists Parīkṣit, Sudhanvan, Jahnu and others. The Agni and Harivamsa put Parikṣit directly after Kuru, and the Garuda and Visnu texts name Jahnu. So the heir after Kuru is Parikṣit in some texts and Jahnu in others.\", \"cites\": [\"l:8147\", \"g:1\", \"v:matsya:MatsP 50.23\", \"v:bhp9:BhP 9.22.4\", \"v:agni:AgP 277.27\", \"v:hv:HV 23.109\", \"v:vip4:ViP 4,19.78\"]}, {\"kind\": \"new_link\", \"text\": \"Raychaudhuri says Janamejaya was Parikṣit's son and calls Bhīmasena his brother. The Agni and Harivamsa verses make Janamejaya the son of Parikṣit in the Kuru line. In the same texts a Bhīmasena appears several generations later, as son of Ṛkṣa (II). The brother Bhīmasena in Raychaudhuri's claims is therefore probably a different man from the later Bhīmasena of the genealogy, and the machine-tied names may mix two persons.\", \"cites\": [\"r:phai1923:15:14\", \"r:phai1923:21:13\", \"v:agni:AgP 277.31\", \"v:hv:HV 23.110\", \"v:agni:AgP 277.34\"]}, {\"kind\": \"disagreement\", \"text\": \"The Mahabharata and the Puranas give Pratīpa's place in the line differently. The Mahabharata names Bhīmasena's son as Paryaśravas and says he is also called Pratīpa, so this is one person under two names. The Agni, Bhagavata, Garuda, Harivamsa, Matsya and Visnu texts name Pratīpa directly after Bhīmasena or Dilīpa. The Mahabharata thus puts Bhīmasena immediately before Pratīpa, while the Garuda, Bhagavata, Matsya and Visnu texts put Dilīpa between them.\", \"cites\": [\"a:mbh1:MBh 1,90.45\", \"v:mbh1:MBh 1,90.45\", \"v:garuda1:GarP 1,140.33\", \"v:vip4:ViP 4,20.8\", \"v:matsya:MatsP 50.38\"]}, {\"kind\": \"open_question\", \"text\": \"Where the genealogy reaches Pāṇḍu's sons and Dhṛtarāṣṭra's hundred sons, the texts name different children. Raychaudhuri says the Pāṇḍavas are shown as scions of the Kuru royal family. Which sons does each text put in the line, and how are those who appear under the same name in different texts to be matched?\", \"cites\": [\"g:19\", \"g:20\", \"r:phai1923:51:8\", \"v:mbh1:MBh 1,90.72\", \"v:hv:HV 24.22\", \"v:matsya:MatsP 50.47\"]}]}",
